---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Privacy and security"
  description="What stays local, what reaches your provider, approvals, and platform shell sandboxes in Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page">
    <p class="kicker">TRUST / DATA FLOW</p>
    <h1>
      Know what moves.
      <br />
      <em>Keep the reins.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel runs on loopback and stores history in SQLite. Model prompts go to
      the provider you configure. Local storage is not local inference.
    </p>

    <div class="data-flow" aria-label="Current message data flow">
      <div>
        <small>YOU</small>
        <strong>Terminal client</strong>
        <span>loopback HTTP</span>
      </div>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <div>
        <small>LOCAL MACHINE</small>
        <strong>V server + SQLite</strong>
        <span>session history</span>
      </div>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <div>
        <small>YOUR CONFIGURED PROVIDER</small>
        <strong>Model API</strong>
        <span>prompt and recent context</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Current protections</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          Backend binds to loopback; remote server binding is not implemented.
          Non-loopback <code>Host</code> / browser <code>Origin</code> values
          are rejected.
        </li>
        <li>
          Provider credentials never leave the API responses. TUI-entered keys
          live in <code>model-settings.json</code> with owner-only POSIX
          permissions (plaintext at rest). Prefer env/keychain policy if
          required.
        </li>
        <li>
          Remote model endpoints require HTTPS; local development may use
          loopback HTTP. Provider redirects are rejected; sizes and message
          counts are bounded.
        </li>
        <li>
          Workspace list/read/search stay inside the session root, skip symlinks
          during traversal, and enforce limits.
        </li>
        <li>
          File edits, shell, and MCP require inspect-then-approve. Application
          verifies the reviewed file snapshot before atomic write.
        </li>
        <li>
          Approved shell can wrap with platform sandboxes (see below). Approval
          remains the authorization gate; sandboxes are defense-in-depth.
        </li>
        <li>
          MCP requires per-session trust plus per-call approval. User-scoped
          servers persist in <code>mcp-servers.json</code> via{" "}
          <code>/mcp add</code> / <code>/v1/mcp-servers</code> (see the{" "}
          <a href={`${basePath}/docs/mcp/`}>MCP guide</a>). Stdio MCP is not OS
          sandboxed. Streamable HTTP refuses redirects and validates TLS.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Shell sandboxes by platform</h2>
      <p>
        Configure with <code>VEASEL_SHELL_SANDBOX</code> (<code>auto</code>{" "}
        default, <code>off</code>, <code>require</code>) and optional{" "}
        <code>VEASEL_SHELL_SANDBOX_NETWORK=allow</code>.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Linux</strong> — Bubblewrap when <code>bwrap</code> is on{" "}
          <code>PATH</code> (<code>shell.sandbox.bubblewrap</code>). Read-only
          host bind, writable workspace, tempfs over sensitive homes, network
          denied by default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>macOS</strong> — Seatbelt via{" "}
          <code>/usr/bin/sandbox-exec</code> (
          <code>shell.sandbox.seatbelt</code>). Best-effort; may be constrained
          by future macOS releases.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Windows</strong> — AppContainer + Job Object (
          <code>shell.sandbox.windows</code>). Workspace ACL for the
          AppContainer SID; job memory/process caps; network denied by default.
          Not Codex-level WFP/LPAC.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Under <code>auto</code>, missing backends fall through unsandboxed and
        do not advertise a sandbox capability. Under <code>require</code>,
        missing backends fail closed. Full policy:{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/SHELL_COMMANDS.md">
          docs/SHELL_COMMANDS.md
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What to keep in mind</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          Your provider receives prompts, conversation context, enabled skill
          instructions, and approved tool/shell output needed to answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          The loopback API has no application token; same-user processes can
          reach health, workspace, and approval routes. TUI review is a
          workflow, not caller-identity enforcement.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sandboxes are not VMs. Inherited environment may still carry
          credentials. Inspect effects before approval.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Threat model.</strong>{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/security/threat-model.md">
        docs/security/threat-model.md
      </a>{" "}
      is the source of truth for attack surfaces. Report vulnerabilities
      privately via repository{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/SECURITY.md">
        SECURITY.md
      </a>
      .
    </aside>

    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/architecture/`}>
        Architecture <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/troubleshooting/`}>
        Troubleshooting <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
